menu "UltraWideLock over-the-air update"

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_DFU_ESP32
	bool "Accept signed firmware updates over Bluetooth"
	depends on BT_NIMBLE_ENABLED
	default n
	help
	  Adds a GATT service that takes a signed application image and writes
	  it into the OTA slot this image is not running from, then points the
	  bootloader at it. The same wire protocol the DWM3001CDK and the
	  standalone FreeRTOS port speak, so one host tool and one web page
	  drive all three.

	  Costs a second OTA slot's worth of nothing -- ota_0 and ota_1 already
	  exist in the partition table -- plus roughly 4 KB of code and the P-256
	  verify path, which the credential stack has already linked.

	  Off by default. This lets a peer in radio range erase a flash slot
	  once an owner has opened the update window, and an image built without
	  a signing key cannot verify anything, so it has to be asked for.

if ULTRAWIDELOCK_DFU_ESP32

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_DFU_LOG_LEVEL
	int "Log level for the update path"
	range 0 5
	default 3
	help
	  Maps onto the ultrawidelock_log.h contract, not esp_log's own levels.
	  3 is warnings and above, which is every refusal and nothing per-frame:
	  a 2 MB image is roughly eight thousand frames, and logging each one
	  costs more time than the radio does.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_DFU_WINDOW_MS
	int "How long the update window stays open, in milliseconds"
	range 10000 3600000
	default 300000
	help
	  Nothing is accepted until the window is opened by hand on the board,
	  and it shuts again after this long. Five minutes is the same default
	  the other two ports use: long enough to walk back to a computer, short
	  enough that a lock does not sit accepting firmware all afternoon.

	  This is an availability gate, not an authenticity one. Authenticity is
	  the P-256 signature over the image header, which is checked before a
	  single byte is written and re-checked by the bootloader afterwards.

endif # ULTRAWIDELOCK_DFU_ESP32

endmenu
